### What is the ELK stack?

The ELK stack is three tools working together to solve the scattered-logs problem in a microservices system. Each service runs in multiple Pods, each Pod writes logs to its own ephemeral filesystem, and a single customer action may touch five services. Without centralization, diagnosing an issue means logging into many Pods by hand.

```text
Microservices                 ELK Stack
┌──────────────┐
│ Transfer Svc │──logs──→ ┌──────────┐   ┌────────────────┐   ┌────────┐
│ Fraud Svc    │──logs──→ │ Filebeat │──→│ Elasticsearch  │──→│ Kibana │
│ Account Svc  │──logs──→ │(DaemonSet)│  │(search + index)│  │ (UI)   │
└──────────────┘          └──────────┘   └────────────────┘   └────────┘
```

### How does Filebeat ship logs in Kubernetes?

Filebeat runs as a DaemonSet, meaning one agent per node, tailing container log files, enriching each line with metadata like Pod name and namespace, and forwarding to Elasticsearch.

```yaml
# filebeat-daemonset.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: DaemonSet
metadata:
  name: filebeat
  namespace: logging
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: filebeat
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: filebeat
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: filebeat
          image: docker.elastic.co/beats/filebeat:8.12.0
          volumeMounts:
            - name: varlog
              mountPath: /var/log
            - name: containers
              mountPath: /var/lib/docker/containers
              readOnly: true
      volumes:
        - name: varlog
          hostPath:
            path: /var/log
        - name: containers
          hostPath:
            path: /var/lib/docker/containers
```

### How does the application produce structured JSON logs?

The key enabler is that the application logs in structured JSON rather than plain text, so fields like service name, log level, timestamp, and correlation ID arrive as queryable fields.

```xml
<!-- logback-spring.xml for structured JSON logging -->
<configuration>
  <appender name="JSON" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
    <encoder class="net.logstash.logback.encoder.LogstashEncoder">
      <includeMdcKeyName>correlationId</includeMdcKeyName>
      <customFields>{"service":"transfer-service"}</customFields>
    </encoder>
  </appender>
  <root level="INFO">
    <appender-ref ref="JSON" />
  </root>
</configuration>
```

```text
{"@timestamp":"2025-01-15T10:23:45.123Z","level":"INFO","service":"transfer-service","correlationId":"abc-123-def","message":"Transfer initiated from ACC001 to ACC002 amount 50000.00"}
```

### What is a correlation ID and how does it flow end to end?

When a request first enters the system at the API gateway, it is assigned a unique correlation ID. That ID is placed into the MDC so every log line carries it, and it is forwarded in the headers of any outbound call to downstream services.

```java
package com.bank.filter;

import org.slf4j.MDC;
import javax.servlet.Filter;
import javax.servlet.FilterChain;
import javax.servlet.ServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.ServletResponse;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.UUID;

public class CorrelationIdFilter implements Filter {

    private static final String HEADER = "X-Correlation-Id";
    private static final String MDC_KEY = "correlationId";

    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse res,
                         FilterChain chain) throws IOException,
                         javax.servlet.ServletException {
        HttpServletRequest httpReq = (HttpServletRequest) req;
        String correlationId = httpReq.getHeader(HEADER);
        if (correlationId == null || correlationId.isBlank()) {
            correlationId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        }
        MDC.put(MDC_KEY, correlationId);
        try {
            chain.doFilter(req, res);
        } finally {
            MDC.remove(MDC_KEY);
        }
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.client;

import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.http.HttpRequest;
import org.springframework.http.client.ClientHttpRequestExecution;
import org.springframework.http.client.ClientHttpRequestInterceptor;
import org.springframework.http.client.ClientHttpResponse;
import java.io.IOException;

public class CorrelationIdInterceptor
        implements ClientHttpRequestInterceptor {

    @Override
    public ClientHttpResponse intercept(HttpRequest request, byte[] body,
                                         ClientHttpRequestExecution exec)
            throws IOException {
        String id = MDC.get("correlationId");
        if (id != null) {
            request.getHeaders().set("X-Correlation-Id", id);
        }
        return exec.execute(request, body);
    }
}
```

### How do you configure Kibana?

You configure Kibana by pointing it at the Elasticsearch cluster, creating an index pattern that tells Kibana which indices to read and which field is the timestamp, and then saving searches and dashboards.

```yaml
# kibana.yml
server.host: "0.0.0.0"
server.port: 5601
elasticsearch.hosts: ["http://elasticsearch:9200"]
kibana.index: ".kibana"
```

```bash
# Create an index pattern via the Kibana API
curl -X POST "http://kibana:5601/api/saved_objects/index-pattern" \
  -H "kbn-xsrf: true" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "attributes": {
      "title": "logs-*",
      "timeFieldName": "@timestamp"
    }
  }'
```

### How do you trace a failed request in Kibana?

When Rajesh reports a failed transfer, the engineer finds the correlation ID from the error response, types it into Kibana, and sees every log line from every service for that one request.

```text
Kibana Discover filter:
  correlationId: "abc-123-def"

Results:
  10:23:45.123 transfer-service  INFO  Transfer initiated ACC001→ACC002 50000.00
  10:23:45.200 fraud-service     INFO  Fraud check passed for abc-123-def
  10:23:45.350 account-service   INFO  Debit ACC001 50000.00
  10:23:45.400 account-service   INFO  Credit ACC002 50000.00
  10:23:45.450 transfer-service  INFO  Transfer completed abc-123-def
```

### How does correlation ID relate to distributed tracing?

Correlation IDs in logs give you the thread to pull, but full distributed tracing tools like Jaeger and Zipkin add timing and parent-child span relationships. A mature setup uses the W3C `traceparent` header so the trace ID and the log correlation ID are the same value.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import io.micrometer.tracing.Tracer;
import org.slf4j.MDC;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class TraceCorrelationBridge {

    private final Tracer tracer;

    public TraceCorrelationBridge(Tracer tracer) {
        this.tracer = tracer;
    }

    public void syncTraceToMdc() {
        if (tracer.currentSpan() != null) {
            String traceId = tracer.currentSpan().context().traceId();
            MDC.put("correlationId", traceId);
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is logging sensitive data into Elasticsearch, where it becomes broadly searchable.

```java
// WRONG: logging full account number
log.info("Transfer from {} to {} amount {}",
    fullAccountNumber, targetAccount, amount);

// CORRECT: mask sensitive data
log.info("Transfer from ***{} to ***{} amount {}",
    fullAccountNumber.substring(fullAccountNumber.length() - 4),
    targetAccount.substring(targetAccount.length() - 4),
    amount);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer manages index lifecycle so logs are retained as long as compliance requires and expired to control cost. They never log passwords, full account numbers, or PII into Elasticsearch. They tie the correlation ID to the distributed trace ID so engineers can jump from a slow trace straight to the matching logs.
